{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01UAfsifDXZC2mFZ275HSj7C","type":"tool_result","content":"=============== translation/Cargo.toml\n     1\t[package]\n     2\tname = \"lib\"\n     3\tversion = \"0.1.0\"\n     4\tedition = \"2021\"\n     5\t\n     6\t[lib]\n     7\tname = \"doubleneg_lib\"\n     8\tcrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n     9\tpath = \"src/lib.rs\"\n    10\t\n    11\t[profile.release]\n    12\tpanic = \"abort\"\n    13\t\n    14\t[workspace]\n=============== translation/src/buffer.rs\n     1\t//! Translation of the buffer helpers `find_value_in_buffer` and\n     2\t//! `create_numeric_buffer` from `c_src/src/lib.c`.\n     3\t\n     4\tuse core::ffi::c_char;\n     5\tuse core::ffi::c_int;\n     6\t\n     7\t/// Equivalent of C `memchr`.\n     8\t///\n     9\t/// `memchr` interprets its `int` needle as an `unsigned char`, which is why the\n    10\t/// caller passes an already-narrowed `u8`. Returns the index of the first match.\n    11\t///\n    12\t/// A zero `size` never dereferences `haystack`, matching glibc's behaviour when\n    13\t/// the pointer is NULL and the length is zero.\n    14\t#[inline]\n    15\tpub fn memchr(haystack: *const c_char, needle: u8, size: usize) -> Option<usize> {\n    16\t    if size == 0 {\n    17\t        return None;\n    18\t    }\n    19\t\n    20\t    // SAFETY: the caller guarantees `haystack` points to at least `size`\n    21\t    // readable bytes, exactly as the C code guarantees to `memchr`.\n    22\t    let bytes = unsafe { core::slice::from_raw_parts(haystack as *const u8, size) };\n    23\t    bytes.iter().position(|&b| b == needle)\n    24\t}\n    25\t\n    26\t/// C: `int find_value_in_buffer(const char *buffer, size_t size, int search_val)`\n    27\t///\n    28\t/// The original narrows `search_val` through `char` before calling `memchr`,\n    29\t/// which then re-widens it and compares as `unsigned char`. The net effect is a\n    30\t/// comparison against the low byte of `search_val`, so `find_value_in_buffer(b,\n    31\t/// n, 300)` looks for byte 44 and `... , -1)` looks for byte 255.\n    32\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n    33\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn find_value_in_buffer(\n    34\t    buffer: *const c_char,\n    35\t    size: usize,\n    36\t    search_val: c_int,\n    37\t) -> c_int {\n    38\t    // `char target = (char)search_val;` -- `char` is signed on x86-64 Linux,\n    39\t    // but only the low 8 bits survive either way.\n    40\t    let target = search_val as u8;\n    41\t\n    42\t    match memchr(buffer, target, size) {\n    43\t        // `return (int)((char*)result - buffer);` -- the pointer difference is\n    44\t        // narrowed to `int` by the original cast.\n    45\t        Some(offset) => offset as c_int,\n    46\t        None => -1,\n    47\t    }\n    48\t}\n    49\t\n    50\t/// C: `void create_numeric_buffer(char *buffer, int size, int seed)`\n    51\t///\n    52\t/// Fills `buffer[i] = (char)((seed + i * 7) % 256)`.\n    53\t///\n    54\t/// Two C details are preserved deliberately:\n    55\t///\n    56\t/// * `seed + i * 7` is `int` arithmetic and can overflow for extreme seeds.\n    57\t///   That is UB in C but wraps in practice, so wrapping arithmetic is used.\n    58\t/// * C's `%` truncates toward zero, so a negative `seed` yields a negative\n    59\t///   remainder, and the subsequent `(char)` conversion wraps it into the signed\n    60\t///   char range (e.g. `132` stays `-124` once reinterpreted).\n    61\t///\n    62\t/// A non-positive `size` simply performs no iterations, as in the original loop.\n    63\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n    64\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn create_numeric_buffer(buffer: *mut c_char, size: c_int, seed: c_int) {\n    65\t    if size <= 0 {\n    66\t        return;\n    67\t    }\n    68\t\n    69\t    // SAFETY: the caller guarantees `buffer` is writable for `size` bytes, the\n    70\t    // same contract the C function relies on.\n    71\t    let out = unsafe { core::slic